**4. CRT / Coppersmith / Boneh decoding**

**KNOWN.** Boneh’s interval construction uses *strong smoothness*. Set
\[
S=\prod_{p\le y}p^{\lfloor\log y/\log p\rfloor}
=\operatorname{lcm}(1,\ldots,\lfloor y\rfloor).
\]
A strongly \(y\)-smooth integer divides \(S\). Boneh treats this and the more general large-smooth-factor problem in [§3, Definition 3.1 and Theorem 3.2](https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/82341411.pdf).

**KNOWN.** The decoding condition has a square-root agreement threshold, schematically
\[
\log T>\sqrt{\log(4h)\log S}
\]
with additional slack, where \(T\) is the required common-divisor size. A checked related interval theorem gives the condition
\[
|I|<2D^{\log D/\log S}
\]
for recovering integers having common divisor greater than \(D\) with \(S\); see [Sarkar–Maitra, §2.2, Theorem 2](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=2b283ed6717f4a323d5076312a6658a53de5509f&repid=rep1&type=pdf). These are conditional recovery ranges, not arbitrary-window constructors.

**PROVED.** Even the generous limiting condition
\[
\log h\lesssim\frac{x^2}{\log S}
\]
fails overwhelmingly here. Elementary bounds suffice:
\[
\log S\asymp_{\text{within log factors}} y.
\]
For a lower bound, a central binomial coefficient divides the corresponding least common multiple: its \(p\)-valuation is at most \(\lfloor\log_p(2m)\rfloor\). Hence
\[
\log\operatorname{lcm}(1,\ldots,2m)
\ge\log\binom{2m}{m}
\ge2m\log2-O(\log m).
\]
The upper bound \(\log S\le y\log y\) is immediate. Consequently,
\[
\frac{x^2}{\log S}\le O(x^2/y)\longrightarrow0,
\]
whereas
\[
\log h\sim\nu F_{2/3}\longrightarrow\infty.
\]

This proves failure of the cited guarantees in this regime. It does not prove that every decoder must fail on these particular inputs.

**KNOWN.** Guruswami–Sahai–Sudan improve weighted and unweighted CRT decoding; the relevant guarantees are [Theorems 4–6, §3.4](https://www.cs.ucla.edu/~sahai/work/web/2000%20Publications/GSS00.pdf).

**PROVED — parameter comparison.** For moduli near \(y\), their unweighted threshold compares approximately
\[
t\asymp\frac{x}{\log y}
\quad\text{against}\quad
\sqrt{\frac{y}{\log y}\frac{\log h}{\log y}}.
\]
The same obstruction appears:
\[
x^2\gtrsim y\log h
\]
would be needed. Here the inequality goes in the opposite direction by an enormous factor. Merely citing improved weighting does not establish recovery below its theorem’s threshold.

**UNVERIFIED.** I found no justified CRT/Coppersmith per-output bound \(L[1/3,\tau]\) for this regime. The available fallback is the interval sieve, with heuristic per-output cost \(L[2/3,a]\). Making the modulus smaller restricts the smooth factors captured; choosing a small modulus that captures a desired solution remains an unresolved search step.

**5. Other constructions and barriers**

**PROVED — deterministic part of a cofactor barrier.** Consider methods that construct a smooth factor \(A\), then choose
\[
t=\left\lceil\frac{kN}{A}\right\rceil,\qquad u=At.
\]
If \(A\le h\), then
\[
0\le u-kN<A\le h,
\qquad
t\ge\frac Nh.
\]
Thus this construction guarantees placement, but leaves an unconstructed cofactor with
\[
\log t\ge x-\nu F_{2/3}=(1-o(1))x.
\]

**HEURISTIC — Dickman behaviour of the remaining cofactor.** Its smoothness probability is still
\[
L[2/3,-a].
\]
Therefore constructing a divisor as large as the window does not change the leading smoothness-search exponent.

**PROVED, conditional arithmetic.** To make cofactor testing cost only \(L[1/3,\tau]\) through Dickman density, one would want
\[
t\le L[2/3,c],\qquad c\le3\kappa\tau,
\]
because such cofactors have smoothness probability
\[
L[1/3,-c/(3\kappa)].
\]
This forces
\[
A\ge N/L[2/3,c].
\]

**HEURISTIC — flat rounding remainder.** For such a large \(A\), placement succeeds with probability approximately
\[
\frac hA
\le N^{-1}L[2/3,\nu+c].
\]
The proposed gain in cofactor smoothness is overwhelmed by the placement search. Serving \(L[1/3,C]\) multipliers cannot cancel an \(N^{1-o(1)}\) loss.

This establishes a barrier for **construct-one-factor, round-and-test methods whose remainders remain unstructured**. It is not a barrier for an algorithm that efficiently engineers exceptional remainders.

**PROVED.** The same issue explains the following elementary routes:

- **Continued fractions / Farey methods:** rational approximation can make \(At-kN\) small, but does not force the remaining integer \(t\) to be smooth. That is the missing condition.
- **Ratios of smooth numbers:** if the denominator divides the numerator, cancellation returns a one-sided smooth integer. Without divisibility, clearing the denominator returns a two-sided relation with a denominator-height constraint.
- **Factorials, consecutive products and binomial coefficients:** constructions using arguments at most \(y\) can force smoothness. They do not, by their defining identities alone, force a small residue modulo \(N\).
- **Base-\(m\) expansion:** smooth monomials do not make their sum smooth. For a monomial \(m^d\), the small-residue condition remains to be solved.

**HEURISTIC — residue flatness.** A polynomial-size or \(L[1/3,O(1)]\)-size family of such explicitly generated values has too few trials to overcome probability \(h/N=N^{-1+o(1)}\). A useful family must exploit an additional correlation with \(N\).

**PROVED — counting-to-search reduction.** An efficient **exact interval-counting oracle** would give a constructor. For instance, compute
\[
\Psi(X+h,y)-\Psi(X-1,y).
\]
If positive, binary search finds the first smooth integer in the interval using \(O(\log h)\) exact count queries. Factorisation can also be recovered by recursively finding a prime cutoff at which the interval count first becomes positive, dividing the interval by that prime and repeating.

**PROVED — limitation of subtracting global approximations.** Under the expected local count \(h\rho\), subtracting two approximations of size \(X\rho\) requires relative error substantially below
\[
h/X=\exp(-x+O(F_{2/3})).
\]
A small relative error in a global Dickman or saddle-point estimate does not give this.

**KNOWN.** Bernstein’s exact enumeration/counting construction includes a meet-in-the-middle count whose work depends on the sizes of the two generated sets; see [§3, Algorithm 3 and Lemma 3.2](https://cr.yp.to/papers/epsi-19950518-retypeset20220326.pdf). It does not establish a cheap exact interval-count oracle here.

**PROVED — a separate log-grid barrier.** For a dynamic program that rounds individual logarithms with error at most \(\eta\), bounds accumulated error by \(s\eta\), and explicitly processes all bins, guaranteeing window precision requires
\[
s\eta\lesssim h/X.
\]
Its bin count up to \(\log X\) is then at least
\[
\frac{\log X}{\eta}\gtrsim
s(\log X)\frac Xh=N^{1-o(1)}.
\]
This applies to that explicit-grid implementation class. It does not exclude compressed representations or a different error analysis.

**UNVERIFIED.** I found no new construction overcoming these barriers. A general lower bound covering arbitrary adaptive lattice, representation, CRT or recursive methods would be a much stronger result than the arguments above.

